    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 4\n    processing_times = [[16, 20, 8, 25], [20, 13, 14, 5], [29, 27, 15, 27], [28, 25, 15, 14]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 0, 3, 1], [2, 1, 3, 0], [0, 1, 2, 3], [1, 3, 2, 0]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Production Planning, Shop Floor Supervisors, and Area Leads\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated Scheduling of Current Jobs Across Specialized Workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. Each customer order moves through a defined routing of operations. Every operation is tied to a specific machine and has a known processing duration. Because different jobs share the same machines at different points in their routings, coordinated scheduling is required to ensure smooth flow and timely completion.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe need to build a feasible, efficient schedule that assigns start times and sequences for all operations across the shop. The core challenge is to align machine availability with each job\u2019s fixed operation order while avoiding conflicts on shared machines. Choices made on one machine can delay downstream operations in multiple jobs, so we must carefully prioritize and sequence work to reduce waiting and idle time.\n\nKey Requirements and Rules\n- Fixed sequence per job: Operations within each job must be completed in the specified order. An operation cannot begin until its predecessor finishes.\n- Machine-specific execution: Each operation must run on its designated machine for a known duration.\n- Machine exclusivity: A machine can process only one operation at a time; overlapping on the same machine is not allowed.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n- Parallelism across machines: Operations on different machines may run at the same time, provided all job-specific ordering requirements are met.\n\nGoal\nComplete all jobs in the shortest possible total time, minimizing the overall completion time (makespan) while keeping machine idle time and inter-operation waiting to a minimum. A good schedule will:\n- Respect all job routings and machine constraints without conflicts.\n- Balance load on shared machines to reduce bottlenecks.\n- Compress the timeline so that all jobs finish as early as possible.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 4\n- Number of machines (workstations): 4\n- Operation processing times: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 4 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 16, Op2: 20, Op3: 8, Op4: 25]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 20, Op2: 13, Op3: 14, Op4: 5]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 29, Op2: 27, Op3: 15, Op4: 27]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 28, Op2: 25, Op3: 15, Op4: 14]\n- Machine assignments by operation (routing): Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 3\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 3, Op4: Machine 1]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 3, Op4: Machine 0]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2, Op4: Machine 3]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 2, Op4: Machine 0]",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Production Planning, Shop Floor Supervisors, and Area Leads\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated Scheduling of Current Jobs Across Specialized Workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. Each customer order moves through a defined routing of operations. Every operation is tied to a specific machine and has a known processing duration. Because different jobs share the same machines at different points in their routings, coordinated scheduling is required to ensure smooth flow and timely completion.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe need to build a feasible, efficient schedule that assigns start times and sequences for all operations across the shop. The core challenge is to align machine availability with each job\u2019s fixed operation order while avoiding conflicts on shared machines. Choices made on one machine can delay downstream operations in multiple jobs, so we must carefully prioritize and sequence work to reduce waiting and idle time.\n\nKey Requirements and Rules\n- Fixed sequence per job: Operations within each job must be completed in the specified order. An operation cannot begin until its predecessor finishes.\n- Machine-specific execution: Each operation must run on its designated machine for a known duration.\n- Machine exclusivity: A machine can process only one operation at a time; overlapping on the same machine is not allowed.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n- Parallelism across machines: Operations on different machines may run at the same time, provided all job-specific ordering requirements are met.\n\nGoal\nComplete all jobs in the shortest possible total time, minimizing the overall completion time (makespan) while keeping machine idle time and inter-operation waiting to a minimum. A good schedule will:\n- Respect all job routings and machine constraints without conflicts.\n- Balance load on shared machines to reduce bottlenecks.\n- Compress the timeline so that all jobs finish as early as possible.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Operation processing times: {PROCESSING_TIMES}\n- Machine assignments by operation (routing): {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}"
